"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2c stage with no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. Absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty as nodal sampling is missing. Biopsy-to-prostatectomy differences are noted as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node removal. The uncertainty surrounding the postoperative PSA and its trajectory is significant due to missing follow-up data. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial for revising this prognosis. The current heuristic sensitivity range for recurrence is between 28 and 65 months. The fixed estimated horizon is 46.50 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.90–65.10 months; its coverage has not been validated."
